{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgMBzsvZ2kWaMR1VxiqXs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JrtEcDw6VVunbaRDAzmYdT","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"fix_prompt":"# Repair plan for /playwright/playwright:`<startup>`\n\n## 1. Diagnosis — why the set fails\n\nThe failing \"test\" is not a test. `<startup>` is the harness's synthetic id for\n\"the suite never got as far as collecting tests\". The discovery artefact\n`tmp/{{RUN_DIR}}/testrun-001-playwright-discover-stdout.json`\nshows exactly that:\n\n```\n\"suites\": [],\n\"errors\": [{ \"message\": \"Error: Process from config.webServer was not able to start. Exit code: 1\" }],\n\"stats\": { \"duration\": 1097.946, \"expected\": 0, ... }\n```\n\n`tests/playwright.config.ts` boots the app under test with:\n\n```ts\nwebServer: {\n  command: 'docker compose up --build frontend',\n  cwd: '..',\n  url: `http://127.0.0.1:${port}`,          // port derived from cwd hash, e.g. 23521\n  env: { FRONTEND_PORT: String(port), COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME: project },\n  timeout: 300_000,\n  reuseExistingServer: true,\n}\n```\n\nand `docker-compose.yaml` declares:\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  frontend:\n    build:\n      context: ./services/frontend\n    ports:\n      - \"${FRONTEND_PORT:-3000}:3000\"\n```\n\n**`services/` does not exist in this repository at all.** Verified with\n`Glob(\"services/**\")` → \"No files found\". So `docker compose up --build frontend`\nfails in ~1 second with exit code 1 (cannot resolve the build context /\nno Dockerfile), Playwright aborts before collection, and the whole suite is\nreported as the single `<startup>` failure.\n\nThis is not a bug in existing production code — the production service has\nnever been written. `docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` declares it as\nexpected, and `CLAUDE.md` confirms it:\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  - name: frontend\n    path: services/frontend\n    language: typescript\n    frameworks: [next.js, react]\n```\n\n> \"Production source lives under `services/`; the frontend Next.js service is\n> expected at `services/frontend/`.\"\n\n**The repair is therefore a greenfield build: create the `services/frontend`\nNext.js App Router service so that the compose build succeeds and the app\nserves the home page.**\n\n## 2. Scope / guardrails\n\n- Write **only** under `services/frontend/**`. This is the sole declared\n  service root, and `.claude/settings.json` allows exactly\n  `Write/Edit/MultiEdit(services/**)`.\n- Do **not** touch `tests/**` (`playwright.config.ts`,\n  `integration/frontend-home.spec.ts`), `docker-compose.yaml`,\n  `docs/**`, `true-bdd/**`, or `templates/**`. The port derivation, the compose\n  build context, and the `3000` container port are fixed contracts that the new\n  service must satisfy — adapt the service to them, never the reverse.\n- Do not weaken, skip, or relax any assertion. The h1 assertion below must pass\n  literally.\n\n## 3. The two contracts the service must satisfy\n\n### Contract A — startup (serves `/playwright/playwright:<startup>`)\n\n`docker compose up --build frontend` must build and then serve HTTP on\ncontainer port **3000**, reachable from the host at\n`http://127.0.0.1:${FRONTEND_PORT}/` with a 2xx/3xx on `/`, within the 300 s\n`webServer` timeout (which covers image build *and* boot).\n\n### Contract B — the home page spec (`tests/integration/frontend-home.spec.ts`)\n\n```ts\ntest('home page greets with Hello world', async ({ page }) => {\n  await page.goto('/');\n  await expect(page.locator('h1')).toHaveText('Hello world');\n});\n```\n\nNote precisely what this demands:\n\n- `/` renders successfully (no error page).\n- `page.locator('h1')` must resolve to **exactly one** `h1` in the whole\n  document. `toHaveText` with a single string against a multi-element locator\n  fails. So: exactly one `h1` anywhere in the rendered document — do not put a\n  second `h1` in the layout, a header, or a banner.\n- Its normalised text is exactly `Hello world` — not \"Hello World\", not\n  \"Hello world!\", not a substring of something longer.\n\nOnce Contract A holds, discovery will stop reporting `<startup>` and will\ncollect this real test, so both contracts must land in the same change.\n\n## 4. Files to create\n\nAll paths relative to `services/frontend/`.\n\n### 4.1 `package.json`\n\nDeclares the app, its deps, and the scripts the Dockerfile calls.\n\n- `\"private\": true`, `\"name\": \"frontend\"`.\n- Scripts:\n  - `\"build\": \"next build\"`\n  - `\"start\": \"next start -H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000\"` — **the `-H 0.0.0.0` is\n    essential**; see the gotcha in §6.\n  - optionally `\"dev\": \"next dev -H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000\"` and\n    `\"typecheck\": \"tsc --noEmit\"`.\n- `dependencies`: `next`, `react`, `react-dom`.\n- `devDependencies`: `typescript`, `@types/node`, `@types/react`,\n  `@types/react-dom`.\n- **Pin a concrete, mutually compatible major rather than `latest`** (a\n  floating `latest` is how this kind of scaffold silently breaks). Next 15.x\n  with React 19.x is a known-good pairing; Next 14.x with React 18.x is the\n  conservative fallback. Whichever you choose, resolve and verify it for real\n  (§7) — do not assume. Keep the React major consistent between `react`,\n  `react-dom`, and the two `@types` packages.\n\n### 4.2 `package-lock.json`\n\nGenerate it by running `npm install` in `services/frontend` and commit it.\nTwo reasons: the Docker build becomes reproducible (`npm ci`), and the\nTypeScript quality gate (§5) needs real `node_modules` on the host to resolve\n`next`/`react` types.\n\n### 4.3 `Dockerfile`\n\nA small, boring, single-stage image. Resist multi-stage / `output: 'standalone'`\ncleverness here; the failure mode budget matters more than image size, and the\n300 s timeout is comfortable for a plain build.\n\nIntended shape:\n\n1. `FROM node:22-alpine` (any LTS ≥ 18.18 satisfies Next 14/15; 22 is safe).\n2. `ENV NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1` — avoids a telemetry network call and noisy\n   first-run output during build.\n3. `WORKDIR /app`.\n4. Copy manifests first for layer caching: `COPY package.json package-lock.json ./`\n   then `RUN npm ci`. (If you deliberately skip the lockfile, use\n   `COPY package*.json ./` + `RUN npm install` — but prefer the lockfile.)\n5. `COPY . .`\n6. `ENV NODE_ENV=production` **after** the install step, never before — setting\n   it earlier makes npm skip `devDependencies`, and `typescript` /\n   `@types/*` are devDependencies that `next build` needs to compile a TS app.\n   This is a classic self-inflicted build failure.\n7. `RUN npm run build`\n8. `EXPOSE 3000`\n9. `CMD [\"npm\", \"run\", \"start\"]`\n\n### 4.4 `.dockerignore`\n\n**Required, not optional.** Must list at least:\n\n```\nnode_modules\n.next\nnpm-debug.log\n```\n\nYou will have run `npm install` on the host (darwin/arm64) to produce the\nlockfile and to satisfy the typecheck gate. Without `.dockerignore`, step 5\ncopies those host-native binaries — notably the platform-specific\n`@next/swc-*` native module — over the correct Linux ones installed in step 4,\nand `next build` dies inside the container with a missing/invalid SWC binary.\n\n### 4.5 `next.config.ts`\n\nMinimal, typed config:\n\n```ts\nimport type { NextConfig } from 'next';\nconst nextConfig: NextConfig = {};\nexport default nextConfig;\n```\n\n(`next.config.mjs` with a plain object and a `@type` JSDoc is an equally\nacceptable, slightly lower-risk alternative.) Do **not** set\n`typescript.ignoreBuildErrors` or `eslint.ignoreDuringBuilds` — that would be\nhiding failures rather than fixing them.\n\n### 4.6 `tsconfig.json`\n\nStandard Next.js App Router config — and note this exact path is what the\narchitecture's quality gate names (`config: services/frontend/tsconfig.json`),\nso it must exist and must typecheck cleanly. Key settings:\n\n- `\"target\": \"ES2022\"`, `\"lib\": [\"dom\", \"dom.iterable\", \"esnext\"]`\n- `\"jsx\": \"preserve\"`, `\"module\": \"esnext\"`, `\"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\"`\n- `\"strict\": true`, `\"noEmit\": true`, `\"esModuleInterop\": true`,\n  `\"skipLibCheck\": true`, `\"isolatedModules\": true`,\n  `\"resolveJsonModule\": true`, `\"incremental\": true`, `\"allowJs\": true`\n- `\"plugins\": [{ \"name\": \"next\" }]`\n- `\"paths\": { \"@/*\": [\"./*\"] }` (optional; only if you actually use the alias)\n- `\"include\": [\"next-env.d.ts\", \"**/*.ts\", \"**/*.tsx\", \".next/types/**/*.ts\"]`\n- `\"exclude\": [\"node_modules\"]`\n\n### 4.7 `next-env.d.ts`\n\nNext normally generates this during `next build`. Commit it (the two standard\n`/// <reference types=\"next\" />` and `next/image-types/global` lines, plus the\n\"do not edit\" comment) so a host-side `tsc --noEmit` is self-sufficient and\ndoes not fail on a missing include.\n\n### 4.8 `app/layout.tsx`\n\nRoot layout — mandatory in the App Router; without it the route tree is invalid\nand `/` will not render.\n\n- A server component (no `'use client'`): the architecture's\n  `component-composition` pattern says \"Use server components by default\" and\n  \"Add `'use client'` only when needed (hooks, browser APIs)\". Nothing here\n  needs the client.\n- Default-exports `RootLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode })`\n  returning `<html lang=\"en\"><body>{children}</body></html>`.\n- May export `metadata: Metadata` with a `title`/`description`.\n- **Must not contain an `h1`** — it would make `page.locator('h1')` match two\n  elements and break Contract B.\n\n### 4.9 `app/page.tsx`\n\nThe home page — the one that actually satisfies the assertion.\n\n- A server component, default-exported `Home()`.\n- Renders exactly one `h1` whose text content is precisely `Hello world`:\n  `<main><h1>Hello world</h1></main>`.\n- Keep it literal. No interpolation, no `{'Hello'} {'world'}` splitting, no\n  trailing punctuation, no extra heading elsewhere on the page.\n\n### 4.10 Optional: `app/globals.css` + import in layout\n\nOnly if you want baseline styling. Not needed by either contract — skipping it\nremoves a failure mode. If you do add it, import it in `layout.tsx` and keep it\nfree of anything that could hide the `h1` (no `display: none`, no zero-opacity\nor off-screen heading) — `toHaveText` tolerates invisible elements, but\n`page.locator('h1')` strictness and future readability both favour a plainly\nrendered heading.\n\n## 5. Quality gate\n\n`docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` declares:\n\n```yaml\nquality_gate:\n  code:\n    - name: typescript\n      config: services/frontend/tsconfig.json\n```\n\nSo `services/frontend/tsconfig.json` must exist (§4.6) and the project must\ntypecheck. After `npm install`, confirm `npx tsc --noEmit` is clean from\n`services/frontend`. The committed `next-env.d.ts` and the real `node_modules`\nare what make this work on the host rather than only inside the image.\n\n## 6. Failure modes to avoid (ranked by likelihood)\n\n1. **Binding to localhost instead of `0.0.0.0`.** If the server listens on\n   `127.0.0.1` *inside* the container, the `${FRONTEND_PORT}:3000` mapping\n   yields connection-refused on the host, and Playwright burns the **full\n   300 s** before failing — a slow, confusing repeat of the same red. Always\n   `next start -H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000`. This is the single most likely way to\n   \"fix\" the service and still see `<startup>` fail.\n2. **Host `node_modules` leaking into the image.** Mitigated by `.dockerignore`\n   (§4.4). Symptom: SWC native-binary error during `next build`.\n3. **`NODE_ENV=production` set before `npm ci`.** Drops `devDependencies`, so\n   `typescript` is absent and `next build` fails on a TS project. Set it after\n   installing (§4.3 step 6).\n4. **More than one `h1`, or inexact text.** Breaks Contract B with a strict-mode\n   / text-mismatch error. One `h1`, exactly `Hello world`.\n5. **Wrong container port.** `docker-compose.yaml` hard-maps to `3000` inside.\n   Do not read `FRONTEND_PORT` inside the app or the Dockerfile — that variable\n   is the *host* port only. The app always listens on 3000.\n6. **Missing `app/layout.tsx`.** `/` fails to render; the dev/prod error page\n   appears instead of your heading.\n\n## 7. Verification the writer should run\n\nFrom the repo root, reproducing what the harness does:\n\n1. `docker compose build frontend` — must exit 0.\n2. `FRONTEND_PORT=23521 docker compose up frontend`, then\n   `curl -i http://127.0.0.1:23521/` — expect `200` and a single\n   `<h1>Hello world</h1>` in the HTML. Tear the stack down afterwards so\n   `reuseExistingServer: true` does not mask a later regression with a stale\n   container.\n3. From `services/frontend`: `npx tsc --noEmit` — clean.\n4. The real thing, from `tests/`:\n   `npx playwright test --reporter=json --config playwright.config.ts integration`\n   — expect `suites` to be non-empty, `stats.expected: 1`,\n   `stats.unexpected: 0`, and no `errors` entry.\n\nStep 4 is the authoritative check: it is verbatim the `replay`/`record`/`live`\ncommand from `architecture.yaml`, and it is what turns the `<startup>` group\ngreen and surfaces `home page greets with Hello world` as a genuine pass.\n\n## 8. Which tests each part serves\n\n| Part | Serves |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Dockerfile`, `.dockerignore`, `package.json`, `package-lock.json` | `<startup>` — makes `docker compose up --build frontend` build at all (the literal exit-code-1 cause) |\n| `start` script with `-H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000`, `EXPOSE 3000` | `<startup>` — makes the URL poll at `http://127.0.0.1:${port}` succeed instead of timing out |\n| `app/layout.tsx`, `next.config.ts` | `<startup>` + `frontend-home.spec.ts` — a valid App Router tree so `/` returns 200 rather than an error page |\n| `app/page.tsx` (single `h1`, exact `Hello world`) | `frontend-home.spec.ts` — `expect(page.locator('h1')).toHaveText('Hello world')` |\n| `tsconfig.json`, `next-env.d.ts` | the architecture's `typescript` code quality gate; also lets `next build` compile the TS sources |","questions":[]},"caller":