t-env.d.ts\\\", \\\"**/*.ts\\\", \\\"**/*.tsx\\\", \\\".next/types/**/*.ts\\\"]`\\n- `\\\"exclude\\\": [\\\"node_modules\\\"]`\\n\\n### 4.7 `next-env.d.ts`\\n\\nNext normally generates this during `next build`. Commit it (the two standard\\n`/// <reference types=\\\"next\\\" />` and `next/image-types/global` lines, plus the\\n\\\"do not edit\\\" comment) so a host-side `tsc --noEmit` is self-sufficient and\\ndoes not fail on a missing include.\\n\\n### 4.8 `app/layout.tsx`\\n\\nRoot layout — mandatory in the App Router; without it the route tree is invalid\\nand `/` will not render.\\n\\n- A server component (no `'use client'`): the architecture's\\n  `component-composition` pattern says \\\"Use server components by default\\\" and\\n  \\\"Add `'use client'` only when needed (hooks, browser APIs)\\\". Nothing here\\n  needs the client.\\n- Default-exports `RootLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode })`\\n  returning `<html lang=\\\"en\\\"><body>{children}</body></html>`.\\n- May export `metadata: Metadata` with a `title`/`description`.\\n- **Must not contain an `h1`** — it would make `page.locator('h1')` match two\\n  elements and break Contract B.\\n\\n### 4.9 `app/page.tsx`\\n\\nThe home page — the one that actually satisfies the assertion.\\n\\n- A server component, default-exported `Home()`.\\n- Renders exactly one `h1` whose text content is precisely `Hello world`:\\n  `<main><h1>Hello world</h1></main>`.\\n- Keep it literal. No interpolation, no `{'Hello'} {'world'}` splitting, no\\n  trailing punctuation, no extra heading elsewhere on the page.\\n\\n### 4.10 Optional: `app/globals.css` + import in layout\\n\\nOnly if you want baseline styling. Not needed by either contract — skipping it\\nremoves a failure mode. If you do add it, import it in `layout.tsx` and keep it\\nfree of anything that could hide the `h1` (no `display: none`, no zero-opacity\\nor off-screen heading) — `toHaveText` tolerates invisible elements, but\\n`page.locator('h1')` strictness and future readability both favour a plainly\\nrendered heading.\\n\\n## 5. Quality gate\\n\\n`docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` declares:\\n\\n```yaml\\nquality_gate:\\n  code:\\n    - name: typescript\\n      config: services/frontend/tsconfig.json\\n```\\n\\nSo `services/frontend/tsconfig.json` must exist (§4.6) and the project must\\ntypecheck. After `npm install`, confirm `npx tsc --noEmit` is clean from\\n`services/frontend`. The committed `next-env.d.ts` and the real `node_modules`\\nare what make this work on the host rather than only inside the image.\\n\\n## 6. Failure modes to avoid (ranked by likelihood)\\n\\n1. **Binding to localhost instead of `0.0.0.0`.** If the server listens on\\n   `127.0.0.1` *inside* the container, the `${FRONTEND_PORT}:3000` mapping\\n   yields connection-refused on the host, and Playwright burns the **full\\n   300 s** before failing — a slow, confusing repeat of the same red. Always\\n   `next start -H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000`. This is the single most likely way to\\n   \\\"fix\\\" the service and still see `<startup>` fail.\\n2. **Host `node_modules` leaking into the image.** Mitigated by `.dockerignore`\\n   (§4.4). Symptom: SWC native-binary error during `next build`.\\n3. **`NODE_ENV=production` set before `npm ci`.** Drops `devDependencies`, so\\n   `typescript` is absent and `next build` fails on a TS project. Set it after\\n   installing (§4.3 step 6).\\n4. **More than one `h1`, or inexact text.** Breaks Contract B with a strict-mode\\n   / text-mismatch error. One `h1`, exactly `Hello world`.\\n5. **Wrong container port.** `docker-compose.yaml` hard-maps to `3000` inside.\\n   Do not read `FRONTEND_PORT` inside the app or the Dockerfile — that variable\\n   is the *host* port only. The app always listens on 3000.\\n6. **Missing `app/layout.tsx`.** `/` fails to render; the dev/prod error page\\n   appears instead of your heading.\\n\\n## 7. Verification the writer should run\\n\\nFrom the repo root, reproducing what the 